<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solomon Mahlangu Freedom College</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1978">1978</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as SOMAFCO</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Mahlangu Freedom College was a school founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> in 1978 at Mazimbu near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morogoro">Morogoro</a> in Tanzania, on land given by the Tanzanian government, to educate young South Africans who had fled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> of 1976. It was named after an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a> fighter hanged in 1979. It closed after the ANC's return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in the early 1990s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tanzanian government gave the land at Mazimbu in 1977, with Anna Abdallah helping to arrange it: some farm buildings on 600 acres. Other names proposed for the school were the Albert Luthuli Institute and the ANC Freedom School. Work began in July 1977 under Oswald Dennis, an ANC civil engineer trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, later joined by the architect Spenser Hodgson; about eighty per cent of the workforce were Tanzanians, while lessons were held under trees or in the old farm buildings. The first dormitory was ready in late 1979, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo">Oliver Tambo</a> opened the college officially in 1985.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The college grew to include the Charlotte Maxeke Children's Centre, opened in 1984 with money from the Swedish Teachers' Union, and a primary school, formally established by 1985. Teaching was in English and joined academic and vocational subjects with political education. By the late 1980s pupils took GCE O level examinations set in the United Kingdom, while development of societies, literature and history of the struggle were examined internally. The ANC stressed the sciences, which black pupils were seldom taught under Bantu Education. Teachers were ANC members and foreign volunteers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and Tanzania.</p><p>Farms supplied milk, eggs, pork, beef and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and teachers and students worked on them each week. The ANC-Holland Solidarity Hospital opened on 1 May 1984, and the Vuyisile Mini Furniture Factory, financed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, in 1980. Scandinavian donors paid for much of the work, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> agencies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzania-peoples-defence-force">Tanzania People's Defence Force</a> guarded the complex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The college closed on 9 September 1992, when it comprised pre-primary, primary and secondary schools, the farm, the furniture factory and other units. Its buildings were taken into the Sokoine University of Agriculture. The Batlagae Trust, set up in 1991 by the National Co-ordinating Committee for the Return of Exiles, gave bursaries that brought the pupils into South African schools. The college's archives went to the University of Fort Hare from September 1992 and were moved to the Howard Pim Library in 1995.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solomon Mahlangu Freedom College” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tanzania was the principal rear base of the southern African liberation struggle: FRELIMO was founded in Dar es Salaam in 1962, and the ANC ran its <b>Solomon Mahlangu Freedom College</b> at Mazimbu near Morogoro.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
